  "name": "Distribution and Abundance of Intertidal Invertebrates at Olsen Bay in Prince William Sound, Alaska, One Year after the 1964 Earthquake",
  "description": "This book references geographic distribution data of algal species: Fucus distichus and Zostera marina and invertebrate species: Evasterias troschelii, Littorina scutulata, Littorina sitkana, Macoma inconspicua, Macoma inquinata, Macoma nasuta, Mya arenaria, Protothaca staminea, Pycnopodia helianthoides, and Saxidomus giganteus and population abundance data of algal species: Fucus distichus and Zostera marina and invertebrate species: Clinocardium nuttali, Evasterias troschelii, Littorina scutulata, Littorina sitkana, Macoma inconspicua, Macoma inquinata, Macoma nasuta, Mya arenaria, Protothaca staminea, Pycnopodia helianthoides, Saxidomus giganteus, and Telmessus cheiragonus from Olsen Bay, Prince William Sound, Alaska. The aforementioned data are referenced in citation: Hubbard, J. D. 1971. Distribution and abundance of intertidal invertebrates at Olsen Bay in Prince William Sound, Alaska, One Year after the 1964 Earthquake. The Great Alaska Earthquake of 1964. Committee on the Alaska Earthquake of the Division of Earth Sciences National Research Council, National Academy of Sciences, Washington, D.C. 137-157 pp. This literature was collected as part of a metadatabase that represents one or more nearshore resources (from physical attributes such as water and air, to birds and mammals). Each entry is viewable in three formats, 1) a geospatial explicit format (ArcView 3.3) named GIS ArcView 3.3 Near GEM project, 2) a spreadsheet format (Excel 2002 and within ArcView) named GEM Nearshore Metadata Set, and 3) a ProCite database named GEM Nearshore Metadata References, which consists of references included in 1 and 2 above. The ProCite database record number corresponds to the Excel Procite Number. Reference Procite #486 for this literature's metadata. The metadata are maintained in digital format (ArcView 3.3, Excel 2002, and Procite) and are available from the Alaska Science Center, USGS in Anchorage, Alaska. For a detailed description of the three databases and the project, see \"Bodkin, J. L., and T. Dean. 2003. Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project G-030687), US Geological Survey, Alaska Science Center, Anchorage, Alaska.\" available on-line at http://www.evostc.state.ak.us/pdf/GEM_final_reports/030687.pdf.\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: Part of the design effort of the \"Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring\" (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project). Provides a comprehensive historical perspective of locations and types of past studies conducted in the nearshore marine communities within Gulf of Alaska in the form of a geographical information system database.",
  "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/23571959",
